our next speaker julia dr julia penov is  the associate director at the tauber  bioinformatics bioinformatics research  center in university of haifa israel  at tauber  bioinformatics center julia together  with the tauber center team are working  to develop a bioinformatics platform for  analysis of omics data  the platform is constantly developed and  expanded to include state-of-the-art  algorithms and approaches for the  analysis of various types of omics data  including single still single cell  transcriptomics  spatial transcriptomics meta genomics  and whole genome sequencing along with a  whole bunch of other stuff julia it's a  pleasure to have you with us i'll let  you roll thank you  thank you very much let me just uh share  my screen  see how it is  and  let's see can you see my screen or also  my notes  [Music]  we can see your screen just fine  great  thank you  um  so um  just  get ready for this okay um so thank you  very much for the organizers elias  for this opportunity to speak today  and thank you to the audience for your  time and attention i hope  it will be  worth your time  i'm very excited to share with you some  of the improvements and the extensions  that we at tower bioinformatics center  together with the pine biotech have been  working on  for the last several months  today i will focus on the transcriptomic  section of the platform which includes  the  bulk rna-seq dedicated to analysis of  raw rna-seq reads all the way up to  differential expression and pathway  analysis  a separate section on the platform for  analysis of already pre-processed  expression tables which many times are  deposited to the ncbi database and so  can be  uploaded to the platform and analyzed  and the  post processing analysis of single cell  expression data  which can be either generated on the  platform or is provided by the 10x  genomics  or in the ncbi database  and also can be uploaded here to the  platform  so um  before going uh  into the uh  the extensions and the new developments  let me just remind all of us about the  basic steps  of the bulk rna-seq analysis so the  first step is the pre-processing of data  which includes the quality control  filtering out the adapter sequences from  the raw reads and filtering of the  duplicated reads which are mostly  generated at the pcr amplification step  of library preparation  and the next step is the alignment step  where the user needs to decide  which reference should be used it can be  either transcriptome  which are individual transcripts from  the genome annotation file  or the genome where it is possible to  identify newly spliced transcripts  specific to the dataset you are  analyzing and so  specific to the molecular events  which you are investigating  uh after the alignment on the reference  uh we need to uh quantify the expression  of genes or isoforms  and they can be presented either as raw  read counts which are recommended for  further downstream differential  expression algorithms  such as dsec2 or hr or others  or in normalized counts such as fpgam or  rpm  and finally the post-processing usually  includes differential analysis  some  pca analysis or enrichment analysis  and if you're familiar with a platform  then you know that we have two basic  sections to the platform or two basic  steps of the platform  the data upload section  where you are asked to about your data  whether you are uploading reads from  your own computer or from the ncbi  database  uh also the user is asked about the  reference uh whether this reference  genome can be found on our system for  example here you can see  that the genome is found in our system  already  or the user is asked to upload his own  reference  and  another  important uh step is  the experimental putting your samples  into experimental groups  so you are asked to drag the samples  into the correct groups so that  afterwards differential analysis can be  performed  on these  groups of samples  and the next part is the part where the  user is asked to choose the algorithms  and options  and again there are many different  algorithms and options for each step  and we try to help the user by  separating each step into uh different  uh columns of the platform and also for  co and also by color coding these steps  so pre-processing steps which are  optional so are missed in this pipeline  that you see on the screen  and they are color coded in blue when  pressed alignment algorithms are color  coded in pink and quantification of  expression is green and finally the  post-processing steps are orange  and when algorithms have been chosen and  the pipeline is executed  you can turn off your computer  uh  and um  because you will receive a notification  email when the pipeline is finished  and uh if you have seen and worked with  a pi with a platform you know that when  you uh when your pipeline is ready  uh you can download all your set all  your results from this download  folder  and if we look at this folder you will  see  many files  all of which might be important  but at first i think may feel  somewhat overwhelming  so what do we have in this file we have  fastqc reports which will tell us about  the quality of our reads  we have alignment statistic table which  will tell us about the number of reads  successfully aligned to the genome or  the transcriptome  we also have the expression tables raw  tables for our genes and isoforms and  normalized in fpgam tables  in addition we also have the dsep  normalized table with all the statistics  and the pca table and the pathway  enrichment  table  so  to make it more readable and to try to  help the user make order in the files we  now offer quick html graphs and  interactive dashboards  with most relevant graphs and tables  so  this is how now the  the pipeline ends with  links to the html graphs and with the  external dashboard  for example this first  graph this first link will lead you to a  graph  uh with uh wanting qc where you can  check the quality of your reads  and  decide whether the uh  the  pipeline that you chose  was appropriate for your data or whether  you have to clean your data a little  more than you thought was necessary  before  so the other html link will show you for  example volcano plot  and here also you can  decide and get a first impression of  your data and your analysis  and finally the last button will lead  you to an interactive dashboard  and the dashboard has  we think has two main functions at the  moment  first you can more deeply explore your  data and it is interactive  so you can  change the parameters on the dashboard  see what happens to your data when you  change the parameters so for example you  can change the thresholds of the volcano  plot or you can choose the  top differentially expressed genes and  see how they are expressed you can also  see your table and download your table  and  so this is one of the uses that we see  in this  dashboard and the second use that  we think can be useful for the users  is that you can also share your analysis  with your collaborators or other  interested individuals  the dashboard is no longer uh dependent  on the pipeline  and even if you if your collaborators  don't have access to your pipelines or  to the platform you can still share the  link with them and they can download all  the results all the graphs and all the  tables and they can explore your data  together with you  another  potential use of these  new developed dashboards is that  many times  maybe you have run into this problem you  are running actually not one or two  pipelines with the same data but you are  trying out many algorithms with  different parameters so for each data  set you might have 10 or 20  data set uh 10 or 20 um  pipelines  and  keeping track of all the uh  tables and of all your  graphs  can be quite a challenge so a dashboard  or collection of these dashboards  can also simplify things  uh and can make order for uh  for the analysis and for choosing uh the  for choosing the pipelines the  algorithms and the  parameters  so uh this was the dashboard for  differential gene expression and rna  [Music]  section and we also developed the same  similar dashboard for the single cell  uh rna-seq downstream analysis but  before going into the  results  let's just go over the basic steps of  the single cell rna seq analysis and  maybe some of the challenges that this  type of data  actually presents  so in single cell rnac uh  analysis pipeline we have three main  steps of analysis which are  pre-processing  uh integration of uh several samples  and downstream analysis  so let me present each step using one of  our projects  where we investigated the changes in  blood transcriptome in one of the marine  tunicate organisms  during hibernation  caused by drop in water temperatures  so we had five single cell rna-seq  expression tables generated from three  active colonies and two hibernating  colonies  so each  sample consists of  several thousand cells  and  is which are represented by about 18 000  genes  and the first step of analysis was  normalizing the gene expression across  all cells in each sample  and selecting variable features or genes  in each sample  and reducing the dimensions  of the  of each sample  using the pca approach  and the second step of analysis was  merging this different samples  together this is an important step  otherwise known as batch correction and  which was also actually mentioned by the  previous speaker  using  there are different algorithms uh of uh  [Music]  of this batch correction of  uh single cell data  so after correcting for the batch effect  we  cluster the cells and project them on  the two-dimensional plane trying to  sustain the local distances between  cells  and  for each cell cluster  we can find now genes differentiating  this cluster from other clusters  also known as marker genes  and  here in this particular  project we ran into some difficulty  and maybe some of you who are working  with single cell data and especially  with non-model organisms  can relate  and actually uh we found that  the  we found that only a small number of  genes that we found as microgenes  matched the known markers for tunicate  blood cells so we were  quite at a puzzle what to do with these  cells  and finally um  without much success in assigning a  particular name to the cell cluster or  uh we still continued with our analysis  and and we found genes that were  differentially expressed in active  colonies and in hibernating colonies so  so this is how the  actual analysis looked first we did  quality control and looking at the  graphs generated by the  by some of these  models that were by the pipeline  we can see that overall expression  levels or counts  in the hibernating colonies are lower  than the inactive colonies across all  cell types or all cell clusters  also the number of genes expressed in  each cluster is much lower in  hibernating colonies  and when we uh did when we performed  umap  looking at the umap uh projection  of cells in the manifold um  we can see that there are much less  cells in the hibernating colonies  compared to the active colonies you can  also produce  a graph that will show it maybe a little  bit  more clearly if you have many  cells so here's the number of cells  and the  clusters and you can see that the active  colonies have a lot of  cells almost in all  clusters  while the number of cells in the  hibernating colonies are very low you  can see here it's a 4 000 and here it's  just 75  and also the density  of expression per each cluster was very  low in the hibernating colonies with  some clusters such as  11 12 having no  cells from the hibernating colony  the next step was finding the marker  genes for each cluster and using these  markers together with  known markers of blood of tunicate blood  cells to assign a type of cell to each  cluster and again as i mentioned before  this was a major  problem actually for us  and  so for example we identified four good  marker genes for cluster eight  and these genes were highly expressed in  most cells of cluster 8 and were almost  not expressed in other clusters in other  cells in other clusters  however when we tried to compare these  genes to known markers of blood cells of  this tunicate and to its uh close  relatives we could not find any  uh genes that matched  uh  so in usual cases when you're working  with model organisms  uh there are different approaches  to finding the markers and to assigning  cell types  these use machine learning algorithms  and other single cell atlases that are  available for uh model organisms but for  uh non-model organisms this is a bit  more complicated and  sometimes is not possible  at least for now  so finally we could also uh find genes  expressed only in hibernating colonies  and almost not expressed at all in cells  of active colonies  so we found two of these genes  gene four two four eight and gene two  two zero one  so i think i made quite clear that  single cell rnac pipeline genera  generates many graphs and tables which  are all collected in a folder in the  result of the pipeline and we  tried to uh compress them each  model is compressed into its own  uh folder to make  a bit more orderly the result files  however still  we  came to realize that it is still quite  complicated to navigate all these  multiple files especially if you are  just starting your uh  your way into a single cell analysis  and so  we  have also a dashboard where all results  are are collected and are interactive  and where the users can  easily navigate through the graphs and  files changing  slightly the parameters  and we find that this dashboard can be a  huge advantage  uh it looks like unfortunately i don't  actually have enough time to go over the  dashboard but i'm sure we will uh be  able to  put this dashboard online and put the  video online how you can navigate  through the dashboard  and what you can actually see  uh  and again i want to uh just mention that  uh the dashboards can be shared after  the analysis is done  and when you have multiple uh pipelines  analyzing the same data set with  different algorithms and different  approaches you can collect these  dashboards  in a more orderly way  to remember and to come back to and  reanalyze  your data and share your research  with others  so uh  what we imagine for the future and for  the future we imagine that these  dashboards will gain momentum and will  prove to be an excellent instrument for  sharing biological data sets together  with the results for example we already  started some collections of data sets  related to the same topic  and which can become a resources  a resource for researchers  and where  each researcher can just  go  right into the analysis look at all the  graphs look at all the tables download  the tables download the code that comes  with the pipeline  and we believe uh  that these can really  allow researchers to easily navigate  all of the analysis of different models  for the disease for example  and  may allow  progress and quick interpretation of the  underlying  events molecular events  also we are planning to make these  dashboards uh  more encompassing including  adding the option to download data  together with all the code and r and  python so that even analysis not  performed on  on the po on the platform can be easily  uh performed  uh by the researcher himself  and  so we hope that the dashboard will  become valuable tools for our students  and researchers who use our platform  but also more wide community of  researchers who can easily access  these  dashboards these analysis and these  collections  of data  so um  i want to thank again everyone on the  tauber and the pine teams who were  instrumental in all the developments i  have been speaking about  uh pyotr myself  alexander  vadim sharov dr sofia fanatica dr lenny  brodsky professor hanov  thank you very much  and of course  thank you for your time and your  attention and  if i have i have three minutes so if  there are any any questions i'm ready to  take these  all right we've got a couple prepared  for you first of all thank you julie  that was very nice and thank you for  that  information what are the specific  challenges that are needed to be  considered when analyzing single cell  data  i think there are many different  challenges we don't make it easy on you  come on now  sure uh  there are um  many different challenges first of all  there are many different technologies of  generating this single cell data and i  think  one should be at least aware of these  technologies  and  use the algorithms that are appropriate  for the technology that you're using uh  another challenge is really uh  what we call batch effect so different  samples have to be aligned with each  other to be able to integrate them into  one analysis  uh and uh  another challenge is really uh  annotation of the cell types if we're  talking about  uh  some  static tissue and if we're talking about  time course then the challenges are even  more uh i think uh  hard to overcome  sounds like a whole other topic to talk  about exactly  we've got a lot of people learning here  so what are some of the different  techniques instruments uh analytical  techniques that you use to prepare a  scrna sequence data  so i think  basically the techniques can be divided  into two types there is uh  well this is a  you know  this is a very big topic actually when i  come to think about it and uh  uh really uh you know  to be able to answer it i would need  another 10 minutes so  okay  very good well we're about at a great  transition point anyhow so we really  appreciate all that data  and all of that information and we  really appreciate your time here and so  now that we know and heard more about  user-friendly solutions that make  bioinformatics more accessible i'm  excited to see how researchers and  students will utilize these methods in  fact if i'm right your the platform you  presented is already integrated into the  omixlogic portal is that correct  yes i believe it is  okay and that will allow students to  learn and apply these techniques so  julia again thank you so much for your  time and uh we appreciate your your  being here thank you very much thank you  for the time  [Music]
 

